le as that and that any Democrat  in the White would have been subject to  similar strategies yes and as soon as  somebody else gets in their way whether  it's tom daschle or somebody else and  we've already seen in the case of  Dashiell we've seen dashiell compared to  saddam hussein in republican issue  attack ads that have been run so the  tactics and the techniques are the same  I think that you're seeing them only in  use right now at the margins because the  war on terrorism is there similar  vitriolic rhetoric on the on the left no  I think there is a distinction and I  think that as I said you can point to  certain excesses on both sides but there  is nothing like the hate that emanates  from the conservative movement on the  other side that animates so much of  their politics I think there is nothing  like the organization on the liberal  side in terms of discipline you see a  very diffuse you really can't even use  the word liberal movement you see a very  diffuse coalition of interest  on the right you see very much a  top-down organization and you see that  they are very disciplined and they are  always on message so i think they fight  in very different ways and frankly I  mean I think the history of the use of  the political smear I think if you look  back even you know even back before this  the time period i'm talking about in  this book i think it comes out of the  republican party I do going back to  McCarthyism and this is really i mean in  some ways you can look at the 90s as a  second wave of McCarthyism yes ma'am hi  hi I'm Phyllis edgert San Francisco  challenge like you have matured and come  to terms with who you are and what what  you really believe in why thank you oh  I'm tried that must feel really good I'm  happy for you but all the people that  you have maligned what have you heard  from them and who is suing you okay  let's see well I haven't heard I have  written to Anita Hill twice apologizing  personally this book is a public apology  in a pretty major way but back in even  before I was thinking of writing a book  about this in early nineteen  ninety-eight I wrote I wrote a personal  note to Anita Hill and then last summer  when I finished the book I also book was  excerpted and talk magazine and I sent  her that excerpt and and wrote a  personal note to her as I mentioned  earlier she has not known as I mentioned  earlier I did during the height of the  impeachment try to make amends for some  of what I had done in terms of Clinton  and I was so much against the  impeachment I felt I needed to say  something about that and did write an  open letter to clinton then he did  publicly accept that apology at that  time so you know I can understand that  they'll still be some hard feelings and  and I would have him too because certain  you know certain kind of damage can't be  undone  because what I'm what I'm writing now is  really not in the heat of the moment  anymore and some of this damage was done  in the heat of the moment but I do think  there's a there's a I do think that  there's an appreciation particularly  among liberals for the idea of  redemption and so I hope that it will go  down pretty well second part of the  question in any litigation are you plan  on uh yeah okay no no actually no there  never has been and then there isn't in  this book certainly yes ma'am hi hi my  name is Amanda Proctor for Washington DC  and in dealing with politics and  reporters most stories I felt with lies  and exaggerations do you feel that it  makes you a better writer to be able to  tell the truth even though society and  the people crave entertainment over  every dance but are you assuming that  most of the things you read are filled  with lies or half-truths that's what  I've been told how do you know that  wasn't a lie oh it's all that no I think  I think it's a good practice to read  everything with questions take  everything with a grain of salt and I  think that particularly the idea that so  much of politics is now discussed in a  format that does strike a lot of people  as entertainment and with an  entertainment become comes over  statement and sometimes embellishment  than an overly dramatic kinds of  presentations so no I mean I I certainly  wouldn't say that I mean I think that I  have become a more balanced and reliable  person but I wouldn't hold myself up as  as better than anybody else in terms of  my journalism certainly there's a lot of  great journalism that's still practiced  today and so you know I wouldn't say  that at all man we have time for a very  quick final question Doris to hill from  Orleans Massachusetts Cape Cod I'm  listening to you in thinking this is  like a religious conversion that you had  can I ask personally was there something  and that triggered it I mean one event  maybe because what I'm hearing is you  did a lot of notetaking and fact-finding  on the Arkansas project do you deny the  things that you found there that you  investigated yeah I'm sorry to do this  to you okay thoughtful question that we  have about 20 seconds for you to respond  to well yes I mean I think that them as  I was saying earlier the credibility of  the people who told me things when I was  working on that Arkansas project i think  is pretty low and so i think that most  of what came out of that and these  things were spread all over the country  it wasn't just you know the arkansas  project the American Spectator the Vince  Foster rumors and drug dealing all these  things about the Clintons that were just  smears all the things they think we're  wrong and the conversion moment i think  if i have to point to one thing it was  this this issue of consciously telling  an untruth and print knowing this  damaging information about Clarence  Thomas covering it up seeing what I was  capable of seeing where the ideology had  led me and really hitting rock bottom  was the one one moment although they're  a series of other moments and there's  not one aha